1
00:00:00,060 --> 00:00:01,080
Maybe zoom in out a bit.

2
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:02,983
So your book, Choosing Courage.

3
00:00:02,983 --> 00:00:04,864
What's the central thesis here?

4
00:00:04,864 --> 00:00:07,873
The central thesis, I guess going back to where we

5
00:00:07,873 --> 00:00:11,496
started is really that courage is a personal choice and it's

6
00:00:11,496 --> 00:00:12,540
a responsibility.

7
00:00:12,540 --> 00:00:17,007
And it helps to think not about courage as if it's some,

8
00:00:17,007 --> 00:00:19,240
sort of, property. You know,

9
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:23,227
I often say if you do an autopsy of somebody,

10
00:00:23,227 --> 00:00:26,670
you won't find some stock of courage somewhere in the body.

11
00:00:26,670 --> 00:00:28,410
There is no such thing.

12
00:00:28,410 --> 00:00:30,651
So it helps to think about courageous action.

13
00:00:30,651 --> 00:00:34,690
And once you say it's about whether you do something in

14
00:00:34,690 --> 00:00:38,803
those critical moments, you then can assume personal

15
00:00:38,803 --> 00:00:39,925
responsibility.

16
00:00:39,925 --> 00:00:43,453
And in a sense, the thesis is that we don't allow

17
00:00:43,453 --> 00:00:48,158
ourselves to say that any other virtue is just the responsibility

18
00:00:48,158 --> 00:00:48,746
of some,

19
00:00:48,746 --> 00:00:50,258
or that we should do it some of the time.

20
00:00:50,258 --> 00:00:54,655
You know, if you think about fairness or moderation

21
00:00:54,655 --> 00:00:55,710
or kindness,

22
00:00:55,710 --> 00:00:58,752
so many other principles are cardinal virtues.

23
00:00:58,752 --> 00:01:01,979
Those aren't just the responsibility of one of my

24
00:01:01,979 --> 00:01:03,660
10 co-workers or myself,

25
00:01:03,660 --> 00:01:05,116
1/10 opportunities,

26
00:01:05,116 --> 00:01:05,665
right?

27
00:01:05,665 --> 00:01:07,886
I'm not really an honest person, Jim,

28
00:01:07,886 --> 00:01:08,705
you know that.

29
00:01:08,705 --> 00:01:10,810
I leave that to the other guy.

30
00:01:10,810 --> 00:01:12,443
My boss is honest.

31
00:01:12,443 --> 00:01:14,310
That's good enough for the team.

32
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:15,705
Why have we allowed that?

33
00:01:15,705 --> 00:01:16,324
Right.

34
00:01:16,324 --> 00:01:18,870
We wouldn't say that about any of these other traits.

35
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:19,788
So these virtues.

36
00:01:19,788 --> 00:01:22,132
So why do we allow that in the realm of courage?

37
00:01:22,132 --> 00:01:25,755
Frankly, I think we let ourselves off the hook too frequently.

38
00:01:25,755 --> 00:01:27,282
And part of it's because we're afraid,

39
00:01:27,282 --> 00:01:30,250
and so the book talks a lot about how to address fears.

40
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:33,488
And part of it's because we're not very skilled,

41
00:01:33,488 --> 00:01:37,377
and so we see so many screw ups in ourselves and others

42
00:01:37,377 --> 00:01:41,280
when people do try to behave courageously that we conclude,

43
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:42,598
you know, it's just too dangerous.

44
00:01:42,598 --> 00:01:45,233
And so the book is fundamentally about saying,

45
00:01:45,233 --> 00:01:47,283
hey, you got to choose your moments,

46
00:01:47,283 --> 00:01:51,206
but then you have to be willing to take on some risk and you have to

47
00:01:51,206 --> 00:01:54,310
be willing to do the work to increase your competency.

48
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:57,310


